  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3 & =2{x}_{2}+3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(3),$ but $1\\ne 3.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
